AR","distance":0.7146,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Ejiro Evero"},{"season":2023,"team":"JAX","distance":0.7385,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"LA","distance":0.7805,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Raheem Morris"},{"season":2023,"team":"GB","distance":0.8578,"shared":22,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Saints went 6-11 in 2025 and were outscored 383 to 306. The defense did most of the work, finishing 10th in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 27th in EPA per play. Their most distinctive traits were the highest 11 personnel usage and the highest Cover 3 rate in the league.","offense":"The Saints offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in all five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the highest 11 personnel usage and the 2nd highest no-huddle rate in the league. Tyler Shough ran the passing game and leaned heavily on Chris Olave, who drew 28% of the targets.","defense":"The Saints defense was well above average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was EPA allowed per rush, where it ranked 4th, and its weakest was pressure rate, where it ranked 22nd. Bryan Bresee was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Cameron Jordan, who had 10.5 sacks despite playing only 53% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the highest Cover 3 rate in the league.","special":"The Saints were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 71% of field goals, which was the worst in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into linebackers, about 19% of the money on the books. Their linebackers were among the oldest in the league.","players":"The quarterback job was split, with Tyler Shough and Spencer Rattler both getting meaningful snaps. Chris Olave was the clear top target with 28% of the looks and 1,163 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by Alvin Kamara and Devin Neal. On defense, Cameron Jordan led the team with 10.5 sacks and Kool-Aid McKinstry picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Kellen Moore called the offense as head coach and Brandon Staley called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Chargers under Kellen Moore and the 2022 Colts under Frank Reich. Their defense looked most like the 2022 Broncos and the 2023 Panthers, both under Ejiro Evero.","trends":"The offense went from 25th in EPA per play in 2024 to 27th in 2025, about where it was. The defense moved from 24th in EPA allowed per play in 2024 to 10th in 2025, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was 11 personnel usage, which went from 34% to 75%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2025. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}